**Why `'Yes'` is rejected:** `===` is a strict, case-sensitive comparison. `'Yes'` and `'yes'` differ in their first character (`Y` vs `y`), so `'Yes' === 'yes'` is `false`.

**Smallest replacement:**

```js
function accepted(value) {
  return value.toLowerCase() === 'yes';
}
```

`toLowerCase()` normalizes case before comparing, so `'Yes'` and `'YES'` both become `'yes'`. It does not trim, so whitespace stays significant: `' yes'` and `'yes '` are still rejected.

This also accepts mixed casing such as `'yEs'`. If only `'yes'`, `'Yes'`, and `'YES'` should pass, use this instead:

```js
function accepted(value) {
  return ['yes', 'Yes', 'YES'].includes(value);
}
```

